### Creating Threads in Java: Thread Class vs. Runnable Interface

Java provides two primary mechanisms to create multithreaded tasks:

#### 1. Extending `java.lang.Thread`:
```java
class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread running via Thread subclass: " + getName());
    }
}
MyThread t = new MyThread();
t.start();
```

#### 2. Implementing `java.lang.Runnable` (Recommended):
```java
class MyTask implements Runnable {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread running via Runnable: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}
Thread t = new Thread(new MyTask());
t.start();
```

| Comparison Parameter | Extending `Thread` | Implementing `Runnable` |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Inheritance Support** | Cannot extend any other class (single inheritance limit). | Free to extend another class while implementing `Runnable`. |
| **Design Paradigm** | Couples task definition directly to thread mechanism. | Decouples task logic from thread execution (Clean Code). |
| **Thread Pooling** | Incompatible with `ExecutorService` task pools. | Perfectly suited for `ExecutorService` and thread reuse. |

$$\therefore \text{Implementing Runnable is the object-oriented industry standard for thread creation.}$$
